1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,496
Friday, nine at night, the bazaar is loud, and Aziz's bag is lighter than it was.

2
00:00:05,546 --> 00:00:09,672
His phone is gone, with the mobile bank and the marketplace still signed in.

3
00:00:09,722 --> 00:00:16,152
From his sister's phone he calls the bank and says one sentence: lock everything until I have a phone again.

4
00:00:16,202 --> 00:00:20,184
Who in this system is allowed to lock Aziz out of his own account?

5
00:00:20,234 --> 00:00:26,472
In Lesson 33, a refresh that gives a different personal identification number ended in a block.

6
00:00:26,522 --> 00:00:31,824
This lesson opens that block: who can set it, what it does at once, and who can lift it.

7
00:00:31,874 --> 00:00:36,744
The list of who can block is short, and I think its shortness is the design.

8
00:00:36,794 --> 00:00:45,144
Only two: the platform itself, when a refresh gives a different personal identification number, and an operator, with a reason.

9
00:00:45,194 --> 00:00:48,144
No other automatic block exists in this version.

10
00:00:48,194 --> 00:00:53,064
A number that the mobile operator gives to a new person never blocks its old owner.

11
00:00:53,114 --> 00:00:56,040
And a failed face check never blocks anybody.

12
00:00:56,090 --> 00:01:01,584
It only counts an attempt, for the phone number, the device and the personal identification number.

13
00:01:01,634 --> 00:01:10,008
After the first counted failure and after the second, a cool-down starts, and after the daily cap no attempt is possible until the next day.

14
00:01:10,058 --> 00:01:18,072
Only failures that the person or the capture caused are counted: a wrong document, a face that does not match, a poor image.

15
00:01:18,122 --> 00:01:22,296
A failure that the service caused, like a timeout, is not counted.

16
00:01:22,346 --> 00:01:24,168
Back to Aziz.

17
00:01:24,218 --> 00:01:30,600
The operator finds him in the panel and presses block, with the reason: phone stolen, customer request.

18
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:32,520
Now watch what one block does.

19
00:01:32,570 --> 00:01:37,944
It ends every access grant of Aziz, in every app, on every device.

20
00:01:37,994 --> 00:01:44,808
It refuses every request from each of his devices, so the phone in the thief's pocket gets an error, not a balance.

21
00:01:44,858 --> 00:01:48,432
It refuses a registration or a login with his number.

22
00:01:48,482 --> 00:01:51,072
It stops each open proofing session.

23
00:01:51,122 --> 00:01:56,904
It keeps his devices and his identity data, because a block is a pause, not a deletion.

24
00:01:56,954 --> 00:02:00,048
It sends the security notification account blocked.

25
00:02:00,098 --> 00:02:02,904
It opens a case, or updates an open one.

26
00:02:02,954 --> 00:02:07,704
And it writes an audit event and sends an event to the bank's antifraud system.

27
00:02:07,754 --> 00:02:10,680
How can all of that be true at the very next request?

28
00:02:10,730 --> 00:02:17,760
The block makes three writes together: the customer state in Postgres, the sessions in Keycloak, and the outbox.

29
00:02:17,810 --> 00:02:24,648
The gateway also keeps a revocation list in Valkey, and every renewal passes the binding check first.

30
00:02:24,698 --> 00:02:28,800
So the stop is real at the next request, even at the busiest hour.

31
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:35,232
The operator path and the automatic path make the same three writes, and only the actor is different.

32
00:02:35,282 --> 00:02:37,104
Now the case.

33
00:02:37,154 --> 00:02:40,224
A case is the operator's folder for one problem.

34
00:02:40,274 --> 00:02:45,840
It shows the customer, the trigger, the related values, masked, and the comparison notes.

35
00:02:45,890 --> 00:02:54,480
Cases also open for other reasons: data that the customer refused, a stopped re-registration, a link that was refused or expired.

36
00:02:54,530 --> 00:02:56,472
Phone reviews have their own list.

37
00:02:56,522 --> 00:03:03,336
Each case ends with one result: no action, unblocked, link set by the bank, or customer contacted.

38
00:03:03,386 --> 00:03:06,720
On Saturday, Aziz has a new phone with his old number.

39
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:10,488
He calls again, and the operator unblocks him, with a reason.

40
00:03:10,538 --> 00:03:12,432
Only an operator can unblock.

41
00:03:12,482 --> 00:03:18,744
An unblock makes the devices trusted again and sends the notification account unblocked, with no new proofing.

42
00:03:18,794 --> 00:03:21,432
Read that once more: the devices.

43
00:03:21,482 --> 00:03:24,696
All of them, and that includes the phone the thief has.

44
00:03:24,746 --> 00:03:30,096
So the careful operator first revokes the stolen phone, and only then unblocks.

45
00:03:30,146 --> 00:03:35,016
The new phone then joins like any new device, with the checks of Lesson 24.

46
00:03:35,066 --> 00:03:37,848
There is one exception to the easy unblock.

47
00:03:37,898 --> 00:03:43,896
When the block came from a different personal identification number, an unblock does not give back any device.

48
00:03:43,946 --> 00:03:51,552
The customer must log in on a device and pass a full proofing, and only then does the platform record a trusted device again.

49
00:03:51,602 --> 00:03:57,864
That makes sense to me: after a mismatch, the platform does not know yet which face was the real one.

50
00:03:57,914 --> 00:04:03,480
To recap: two can block, the platform on a different number and an operator with a reason.

51
00:04:03,530 --> 00:04:09,000
A block ends every session at the next request, keeps the data, and opens a case.

52
00:04:09,050 --> 00:04:14,328
Only an operator unblocks, and the devices come back, except after a mismatch.

53
00:04:14,378 --> 00:04:17,520
But one thing from Friday night is still strange.

54
00:04:17,570 --> 00:04:24,072
Aziz got account blocked by SMS and by push, and one of his devices was in a thief's pocket.

55
00:04:24,122 --> 00:04:26,304
What can a message on a stolen phone show?

56
00:04:26,354 --> 00:04:28,488
Next: notifications.
